{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Aaron saw.</b> Since the deed of Satan had succeeded, he thought to deflect them by building an altar, for he said that it would not be possible to slaughter to it without an altar. 'And he proclaimed a feast to YHWH tomorrow' — not to the calf but to YHWH, for he thought that tomorrow Moses would surely come, but Satan spurred them on so that they rose early the next morning and did not wait the few hours until day's end. 'And they offered burnt offerings and rose up to play' — our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Tanhuma Ki Tisa 20) this as an allusion to forbidden sexual relations and to bloodshed. And it appears to me, since it says 'to play' and does not say 'and they played,' infer from this that there was not there actual laughter of forbidden sexual relations and bloodshed, but rather because they transgressed 'I' and 'You shall not have,' it is as if they transgressed 'You shall not murder' and 'You shall not commit adultery,' for the commandments were five against five: 'You shall not murder' opposite 'I,' and 'You shall not commit adultery' opposite 'You shall not have.' Therefore 'to play' is stated, for they transgressed 'I' and 'You shall not have,' which bring also to the laughter of forbidden sexual relations and bloodshed, for whoever commits this one falls into that one — since one who accepts idolatry upon himself has already thereby denied the entire Torah, and it is as though he transgressed all its commandments."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:48.409029272Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}